  "id": "Compound::DB00521 - Gene::154",
  "verbalization": "Carteolol is implicated in the pathogenesis of Blepharoconjunctivitis and Eyelid Ptosis through a shared mechanism involving Levobunolol, a beta-adrenergic antagonist. Both Carteolol and Levobunolol bind to Adrb2, suggesting a direct pharmacological interaction that may contribute to the observed clinical effects. The presence of a common lowest ancestor, Beta-Adrenergic Antagonist, underscores a shared biological function, reinforcing the hypothesis that Carteolol exerts its effects via a mechanism involving Adrb2. The evidence also highlights a common structural feature, Organic Aromatic Compound, which may facilitate binding to Adrb2. The similarity between Carteolol and Penbutolol, which also binds to Adrb2, further supports the notion that Carteolol's interaction with Adrb2 is a pharmacological property of beta-adrenergic antagonists. The integrated evidence suggests that Carteolol may act through a mechanism involving Adrb2, consistent with the known pharmacological profile of beta-adrenergic antagonists.",
